## Current State of Skill Lab

The app is a **React + TypeScript + Vite PWA**  with Firebase auth/Firestore for skill storage  and a mobile-first bottom nav. All AI work happens exclusively through a cloud Gemini API key in `src/services/aiSkillAgent.ts`  — every `proposeSkill`, `refineSkill`, `simulateSkill` and `analyzePhoneState` call hits `@google/genai` remotely. There is **zero local model awareness, zero on-device inference, and no phone scanning logic** anywhere in the codebase .

The `types.ts` already has `schedule`, `workflow`, and `testScenarios` fields , and the agent is already designed around Gemma-optimized prompts and on-device hints  — so the intention was always there, the bridge just hasn't been built.

***

## The Core Technical Gap

Edge Gallery does **not** expose a public HTTP API server — this is actually an open feature request on their GitHub. It runs Gemma using the **MediaPipe LLM Inference API** directly inside the Android/iOS app as a sandboxed process. You cannot simply call Edge Gallery's model from another app at runtime without a native bridge. [github](https://github.com/google-ai-edge/gallery/issues/552)

You have two viable paths, and you should do **both**:

| Layer | Path A — PWA/Web (short-term) | Path B — Native Android (full vision) |
|---|---|---|
| Runtime | `@mediapipe/tasks-genai` in browser | MediaPipe Android SDK via Capacitor |
| Model source | User downloads `.task` file into OPFS | Reads Edge Gallery's model from shared storage |
| Phone scanning | Web File System Access API | Native `File` + `ContentResolver` |
| Release target | Runs today in Chrome on Android | Full Play Store app with Edge Gallery synergy |

